Task 1: Access the Kiali Dashboard
Subtask 1.1: Verify Kiali Installation
First, let's confirm that Kiali is properly installed in your cluster.

Open your terminal and check if Kiali is running:
kubectl get pods -n istio-system | grep kiali
Expected output should show a running Kiali pod:

kiali-6f457f5964-xyz12   1/1     Running   0          5m
Verify the Kiali service is available:
kubectl get svc -n istio-system | grep kiali
Subtask 1.2: Access Kiali Dashboard
Create a port-forward to access Kiali locally:
kubectl port-forward svc/kiali -n istio-system 20001:20001
Open a new terminal tab and keep the port-forward running. In your web browser, navigate to:
http://localhost:20001
If prompted for credentials, use the default login:
Username: admin
Password: admin
Note: In production environments, always change default credentials for security.

Subtask 1.3: Explore the Kiali Interface
Once logged in, familiarize yourself with the main navigation menu:

Overview: High-level view of all namespaces
Graph: Visual representation of service mesh topology
Applications: Application-centric view
Workloads: Kubernetes workload information
