Take note of the time range selector in the top-right corner, which allows you to view historical data.

Task 2: View Service-to-Service Communication
Subtask 2.1: Deploy Sample Application
Deploy the Bookinfo sample application to generate traffic:
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio/release-1.20/samples/bookinfo/platform/kube/bookinfo.yaml
Enable Istio sidecar injection for the default namespace:
kubectl label namespace default istio-injection=enabled
Verify the application pods are running:
kubectl get pods
Wait until all pods show 2/2 in the READY column (indicating both the application container and Istio sidecar are running).

Subtask 2.2: Create Istio Gateway
Create an Istio Gateway to expose the application:
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio/release-1.20/samples/bookinfo/networking/bookinfo-gateway.yaml
Get the ingress gateway external IP:
kubectl get svc istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system
Subtask 2.3: Generate Traffic
Create a script to generate continuous traffic:
cat > generate-traffic.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
GATEWAY_URL=$(kubectl get svc istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip}')
if [ -z "$GATEWAY_URL" ]; then
    GATEWAY_URL="localhost"
    kubectl port-forward svc/istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system 8080:80 &
    PORT_FORWARD_PID=$!
    GATEWAY_URL="localhost:8080"
fi

echo "Generating traffic to $GATEWAY_URL"
for i in {1..100}; do
    curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage" > /dev/null
    echo "Request $i sent"
    sleep 2
done
EOF
Make the script executable and run it:
chmod +x generate-traffic.sh
./generate-traffic.sh &
